          "ruleId": "CWE-79",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Attacker-controlled OBJECT PROPERTY NAMES are never sanitized. In lib/sanitize.js:42 the object branch does `Object.keys(data).forEach((key) => { ... data[key] = sanitizeHtml(item, ...) ... })` -- only the VALUE at each key is passed through sanitizeHtml; the key string itself is never rewritten or validated. Attacker input reaches here from index.js:9 (`req[k] = sanitize(req[k], options)` for body/params/headers) and index.js:12 (`sanitize(req.query, options)`). So a request body/query of the form {\"<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>\": \"benign\"} passes completely through the middleware with the HTML payload intact in the key. Any consuming app that renders or echoes request-object keys (e.g. iterating req.body to build an HTML form/table, or serializing the object into a template) gets stored/reflected XSS despite the sanitizer being installed. This defeats the library's stated contract that everything in req is XSS-safe.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently from source. In lib/sanitize.js:41-53 the object branch iterates Object.keys(data) and sanitizes only the VALUE (data[key] = sanitizeHtml(item, ...) at line 48, or recurses at line 50); the key string is used solely as a subscript and in the allowedKeys.includes(key) check (grep: key appears only at lines 42,43,46,48,50). It is never escaped, rewritten, or deleted, and the object is mutated in place, so attacker-supplied property names pass through verbatim. Graph confirms only three sanitizeHtml call sites exist in the entire scan (lib/sanitize.js:28 on `data`, :33 on `item`, :48 on `item`) and none receives `key`. Reachability holds despite the graph marking index.js:9/12 reachable_from_entry=false (arrow-function under-linking of the middleware closure returned by middleware()): source shows index.js:9 passes req.body/params/headers and index.js:12 passes req.query directly into sanitize, and `xss` is the exported entry at index.js:24; lib/sanitize.js:42 itself is reachable_from_entry=true, hop=1. Exploit: POST body {\"<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>\":\"benign\"} or GET ?<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>=1 returns with the payload intact in the key, defeating README line 2's claim that req.body/query/headers/params are sanitized against XSS. The 1317-line test suite asserts only on values and has no key-sanitization test, so this is an untested gap rather than a documented limitation. Severity held at MEDIUM rather than HIGH because this is a library with no rendering sink of its own: XSS fires only in consumers that echo key names (field dumps, dynamic form/table builders, \"unknown field: X\" errors), making it an incomplete-sanitization failure of a security control. Advisory exploit-reference check was unavailable (exploit corpus not indexed), and dependency versions are ranges only (sanitize-html ~2.13.0, no lockfile) \u2014 immaterial, as the defect is in this repo's own code, not a dependency CVE."
          },
