        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-674",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Unbounded attacker-controlled recursion depth -> stack-exhaustion DoS. The `sanitize` function recurses at lib/sanitize.js:36 (`sanitize(options, item)` inside the Array.map branch) and lib/sanitize.js:50 (`sanitize(options, item)` inside the object branch) with NO depth counter, NO visited-set, and NO cycle guard -- the function's only parameters are `options` and `data`. Attacker fully controls nesting depth of a JSON body (body-parser is a declared dependency) or of a bracketed query string, so a request like {\"a\":{\"a\":{\"a\": ... }}} nested a few thousand levels deep drives recursion past the V8 stack limit, throwing a RangeError inside the synchronous middleware before next() at index.js:19 -- an unhandled exception per request, giving cheap remote DoS of every route behind the middleware.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently confirmed the structural defect: `sanitize` (lib/sanitize.js:26) takes only `(options, data)` and recurses unconditionally at line 36 (inside the `data.map` arrow) and line 50 (inside the `Object.keys(...).forEach` arrow) with no depth counter, no visited set and no cycle guard \u2014 a repo-wide grep for `depth|WeakSet|Set(|seen|visited|maxDepth` finds zero guards in index.js/lib/sanitize.js, and the graph reports the method's parameters as exactly [this:0, options:1, data:2]. The sink is genuinely reachable: index.js:5-21 returns a synchronous middleware that calls sanitize on req.body (line 9) and req.query (line 12) before next() (line 19); the graph has EntryPoint{kind:handler, exposure:reachable} -> index.js::program:middleware and both recursion calls at reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=2. Attacker controls nesting depth via a JSON body in the documented deployment (bodyParser.json() + xss(), test/test.js:17-19, README.md:23-25): at body-parser's default 100 kb a nested-array payload reaches ~50k levels while sanitize consumes ~3 JS frames per level (sanitize -> map/forEach -> arrow -> sanitize), overflowing Node's ~11k-frame stack at ~3-4k levels, so a depth window where the body parses but sanitize throws RangeError always exists. Two of the analyst's sub-claims are WRONG and I corrected them: (1) the bracketed query-string vector is not viable because qs enforces depth:5 by default; (2) it is not an \"unhandled exception\" \u2014 Express's Layer.handleRequest try/catches synchronous middleware and forwards to next(err), so the result is a 500 per request plus CPU/heap amplification, not a process crash. Also, body-parser/express are devDependencies (package.json:36-47), not runtime deps, and the README's own `limit:'1kb'` example would cap depth near ~500 levels and defeat the attack \u2014 so impact is deployment-dependent. CWE-674 confirmed; severity downgraded to MEDIUM."
          },
          "locations": [
