        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-1188",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Fail-open on the strictest configuration: initializeOptions() only forwards allowedTags into sanitizerOptions when `options.allowedTags.length > 0`, and only forwards allowedAttributes when `Object.keys(options.allowedAttributes).length > 0`. A developer who explicitly requests the MOST restrictive policy -- `allowedTags: []` (strip every tag) or `allowedAttributes: {}` (strip every attribute) -- has that setting silently discarded, leaving sanitizerOptions without the key, so sanitize-html falls back to its built-in permissive default allowlist instead of stripping everything. The requested control is silently disabled rather than enforced; the app believes all HTML is removed while a default tag/attribute allowlist is actually in force.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source. lib/sanitize.js:12-18 gates forwarding on `options.allowedTags.length > 0` and `Object.keys(options.allowedAttributes).length > 0`, so the two strictest possible policies -- `allowedTags: []` (strip all tags) and `allowedAttributes: {}` (strip all attributes) -- are silently dropped and never reach sanitize-html; lines 20-23 show no other path can set them, and sanitizerOptions goes unmodified into sanitizeHtml() at lines 28/33/48. The resulting fallback is not theoretical: the project's own test at test/test.js:1228-1274 exercises `sanitizerOptions === {}` and asserts that `<p onclick>`, `<h1 onclick>`, `<h6>` and `<a href=\"/\">` all survive -- exactly the state a caller requesting \"strip everything\" is silently placed in, with sanitize-html's built-in default allowlist in force. No doc or test sanctions empty-as-defaults (grep for `allowedTags: []` / `allowedAttributes: {}` across the repo: zero matches), and README.md:38-45 presents allowedTags purely as an allowlist, so `[]` naturally reads as \"allow nothing\". Reachability is the package's public API (index.js:5-21 `xss()` middleware over req.body/params/headers/query, plus exported `sanitize` at index.js:25); the graph's reachable_from_entry=false on index.js:9/:12 is the known arrow-function under-linking artifact (sibling rows at index.js:5 and lib/sanitize.js:6 are reach:true) and does not downgrade a library whose entry point is the exported middleware. Severity held to MEDIUM rather than HIGH because the fallback is sanitize-html's *default* allowlist, which still strips <script>, <img> and on* handlers (same test: a:'', c:'', onclick removed) and limits href to safe schemes -- so the realized impact is HTML injection / content spoofing and the silent disabling of an explicitly requested control, not direct script execution. Advisory exploit_search could not be completed (corpus reports \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\"); irrelevant here since the defect is in this repo's option plumbing, not in sanitize-html ~2.13.0 itself."
          },
